      ":question/problem": "Given that $y=\\sqrt{-8{x}^{2}-6x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-8{x}^{2}-6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-16x-6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-16x-6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(8x+3)}{2\\sqrt{-8{x}^{2}-6x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8x+3}{\\sqrt{-8{x}^{2}-6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
